<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Pinya Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1313 CE – 1365 CE · capital Pinya</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A short-lived central Burmese kingdom founded by Thihathu in 1313, rival of Sagaing and forerunner of the Ava Kingdom.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thihathu">Thihathu</a>, the last surviving of the three <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/myinsaing-kingdom">Myinsaing</a> brothers, founded a new capital at Pinya near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyaukse">Kyaukse</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> lands in 1313 and proclaimed himself heir to the Pagan kings. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saw-yun">Saw Yun</a> soon broke away and set up a rival court at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sagaing-kingdom">Sagaing</a> in 1315.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Pinya ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyaukse">Kyaukse</a> granary and parts of central <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/myanmar">Myanmar</a>, but its kings struggled with rebellious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>, the breakaway <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sagaing-kingdom">Sagaing Kingdom</a> and Shan raids from the north. Royal patronage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> continued, and the court maintained Pagan-era titles and administration on a smaller scale. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzana-i-of-pinya">Uzana I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyawswa-i-of-pinya">Kyawswa I</a> were among its kings.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1364 Shan forces from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mong-mao">Mong Mao</a> sacked both Pinya and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sagaing-kingdom">Sagaing</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thado-minbya">Thado Minbya</a>, a prince of the Sagaing line linked to the Pinya house, reunited the region and in 1365 founded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ava-kingdom">Ava Kingdom</a> at a new capital nearby, which absorbed Pinya.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/pinya-kingdom.jpg" alt="Pinya Kingdom" width="320" height="634"><figcaption>Image: Hybernator · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Burma_c._1350.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1313 CE – 1365 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Pinya</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Thihathu, Uzana I, Kyawswa I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Theravada Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Burmese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Pinya</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1339&amp;focus=pinya-kingdom" aria-label="Pinya Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7255 1370 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Pinya Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7575 1626L7574 1626M7575 1626L7574 1625"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7574 1626h0M7574 1625h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7575 1626h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7575 1626h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Pinya. <a href="/atlas?year=1339&amp;focus=pinya-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
